    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "spain",
      "title": "Future or ir a?",
      "text": "In everyday Spanish the <b>ir a + infinitive</b> (A2 PDF 6) is more common for plans: <i>Voy a llamarte mañana.</i> The simple future is preferred for <b>promises, predictions, formal language and probability</b>. After <b>si</b> (if), never use the future for the if-clause: <i>Si tengo tiempo, iré.</i>"
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "zal = futuro, zou = condicional",
      "text": "Dutch <i>zal</i> (will) matches the Spanish future, and Dutch <i>zou</i> (would) matches the conditional. Dutch often uses the present for the future (<i>Morgen werk ik</i>), and so does Spanish (<i>Mañana trabajo</i>)."
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "4. The conditional"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Take the same stem as the future (the whole infinitive, or the irregular stem) and add the <b>imperfect -er / -ir endings</b>: <b>-ía, -ías, -ía, -íamos, -íais, -ían</b>. The endings are the same for all three verb groups."
    },
